Food-prep — Development Roadmap
Roadmap principle
Do not expand the ingredient catalogue aggressively yet.
The next work should prove the complete product loop using a small number of ingredients. Additional data only becomes valuable after the journey, explanation and hypothesis systems work.
The recommended development order is:
clarify the model → build one complete journey → build one hypothesis engine → test in the kitchen → expand carefully

Phase 0 — Preserve the current foundation
Goal
Protect the useful work already present before restructuring the product.
Work
    • Keep the current SQLite schema, YAML loader, query engine, tests, CLI and Streamlit tabs working.
    • Label the existing UI internally as the Workshop or Curator interface.
    • Document current concepts and known limitations.
    • Add a short architectural decision record stating that Cook and Scout are equally important product modes.
    • Avoid major schema deletion until the new journey model has been tested.
Deliverables
    • docs/PRODUCT_DESIGN.md
    • docs/ROADMAP.md
    • docs/adr/dual-mode-cook-scout.md
    • Current test suite passing.
Exit condition
The project can change direction without losing the existing ontology inspection tools.

Phase 1 — Define the common language
Goal
Create a controlled vocabulary for flavour, texture, preparation, transformation, destination and correction.
Work
Define a compact initial vocabulary.
Preparation
    • whole
    • halved
    • sliced
    • chopped
    • finely chopped
    • shredded
    • grated
    • crushed
    • separated
Transformation
Keep the existing techniques, but clarify which are:
    • preparation modifiers;
    • primary state changes;
    • preservation processes;
    • secondary transformations.
Flavour
Start with approximately ten dimensions:
    • salty
    • sour
    • sweet
    • bitter
    • umami
    • pungent
    • aromatic
    • nutty/toasted
    • fresh/green
    • rich/fatty
Texture
Start with approximately ten dimensions:
    • crisp
    • crunchy
    • tender
    • soft
    • silky
    • juicy
    • dry
    • chewy
    • sticky
    • creamy
Destinations
Start with:
    • side
    • complete plate
    • rice or grain bowl
    • noodles
    • soup
    • salad
    • toast or sandwich
    • filling
    • sauce or base
    • condiment
    • preserve for later
Corrections
Define common sensory problems and suitable responses.
Deliverables
    • Vocabulary YAML files.
    • Validation rules.
    • Documentation defining each term.
    • Tests preventing unknown or duplicate vocabulary values.
Exit condition
The same language can describe both reliable cooking routes and Scout hypotheses.

Phase 2 — Model one complete ingredient journey
Goal
Prove that Food-prep can tell a coherent cooking story.
Recommended ingredient
Broccoli or cabbage.
Broccoli is suitable because its stems and florets create different preparation paths.
Cabbage is suitable because it supports raw, salted, fried, braised, roasted, pickled and fermented states.
Choose one and finish it before expanding.
Work
For the chosen ingredient, define:
    • preparation options;
    • transformations;
    • resulting states;
    • sensory changes;
    • risks;
    • next transitions;
    • flavour routes;
    • corrections;
    • destinations.
Build approximately four complete journeys.
Example broccoli journeys:
    1. sliced stems → stir-fry → soy and garlic → vinegar finish → rice or noodles;
    2. florets → roast → sour and nutty route → side or grain bowl;
    3. florets → steam → cool and dress → cold vegetable side;
    4. roasted broccoli → crush → sauce, spread or filling.
User-facing story requirements
Each journey must explain:
    • why to choose it;
    • what changes;
    • what becomes possible;
    • what can go wrong;
    • what can be added;
    • where it leads.
Deliverables
    • New journey data structures.
    • Ingredient Journey query endpoint.
    • CLI rendering for journeys.
    • Tests for four end-to-end paths.
    • No new consumer UI required until the text output is convincing.
Exit condition
A user can start with one ingredient and follow a complete path without reading raw tags or database terminology.

Phase 3 — Add destination-aware reasoning
Goal
Stop treating every component as though it must become the same generic balanced plate.
Work
Create destination profiles.
Each destination should define:
    • essential functions;
    • useful functions;
    • optional functions;
    • unsuitable additions;
    • typical texture needs;
    • typical moisture needs.
Examples:
Side dish
May require:
    • seasoning;
    • one balancing flavour;
    • suitable texture.
Does not inherently require carbohydrate and protein.
Complete plate
May require:
    • satisfying anchor;
    • protein or equivalent body;
    • carbohydrate or another substantial base;
    • seasoning;
    • moisture and contrast.
Soup
May need:
    • broth or hydration;
    • aromatic depth;
    • salt;
    • body.
Crunch may be optional or a garnish rather than a hard gap.
Refactor
Replace the universal TARGET_ROLES assumption with destination-aware targets.
Retain the current plate target as one profile, such as complete_savoury_plate.
Deliverables
    • Destination schema or YAML.
    • Destination-aware gap calculation.
    • Regression tests for side, soup and complete plate.
    • Updated output explaining why something is or is not missing.
Exit condition
The engine no longer tells every dish that it needs the same seven things.

Phase 4 — Unify states and plate profiles
Goal
Make the ingredient journey and plate-balance systems operate on the same culinary objects.
Work
    • Link transformed components to sensory or plate profiles.
    • Remove duplicate naming where possible.
    • Allow any sufficiently modelled transformed state to enter destination or plate reasoning directly.
    • Retain lightweight profiles for foods that do not yet need full ingredient trees.
A transformed component should not need a separately authored shadow entry merely to participate in Plate Balance.
Deliverables
    • Explicit state-to-profile relationship.
    • Migration or compatibility layer for current data.
    • Tests showing that a component produced in Ingredient Journey can be used immediately in destination reasoning.
    • Updated debug views.
Exit condition
The system behaves as one graph rather than two adjacent databases.

Phase 5 — Build flavour routes
Goal
Create reusable culinary structures between individual ingredients and full recipes.
Initial routes
Build approximately six:
    • soy and garlic;
    • sesame and vinegar;
    • ginger and scallion;
    • chilli and umami;
    • sweet and sour;
    • toasted nut and sharp fruit.
Each route should define:
    • flavour contribution;
    • suitable transformed states;
    • common supporting ingredients;
    • possible finishes;
    • destinations;
    • known risks;
    • cultural context;
    • confidence.
Work
    • Add route matching to Cook.
    • Explain why a route suits a state.
    • Let available ingredients partially or fully satisfy a route.
    • Allow substitution within a route while preserving function.
Deliverables
    • Flavour-route data model.
    • Route selection query.
    • Available-ingredient route matching.
    • Tests covering reinforcement, contrast and unsuitable combinations.
Exit condition
The system can recommend a coherent flavour direction rather than a loose list of fillers.

Phase 6 — Build the Scout hypothesis engine
Goal
Return to the project’s central research question.
First implementation
Do not begin with machine learning.
Use transparent rule-based candidate generation.
For a transformed state:
    1. identify dominant flavour and texture dimensions;
    2. identify useful contrasts or reinforcements;
    3. identify established ingredients that perform those functions;
    4. locate substitutes with similar functional profiles;
    5. exclude obviously incompatible or unsafe candidates;
    6. check direct recipe occurrence;
    7. rank candidates by compatibility and novelty separately;
    8. generate an explanation.
Candidate evidence
Every hypothesis must include at least one of:
    • analogy substitution;
    • flavour contrast;
    • flavour reinforcement;
    • transformation fit;
    • functional completion;
    • aroma bridge;
    • texture contrast.
Deliverables
    • Candidate hypothesis schema.
    • Compatibility calculation.
    • Novelty observation structure.
    • Explanation generator using structured templates.
    • Rejection reasons.
    • Tests demonstrating that rarity alone cannot create a high-ranking candidate.
Exit condition
Scout can generate a plausible uncommon pairing without relying on a manually written final pairing record.

Phase 7 — Add corpus-based novelty checking
Goal
Measure how common or uncommon candidate combinations appear in available evidence.
Work
    • Select one legally usable recipe dataset or corpus.
    • Normalise ingredient aliases.
    • Record direct co-occurrence counts.
    • Record distinct recipe contexts.
    • Track corpus identity, scope and date.
    • Distinguish zero observations from missing coverage.
    • Add context-sensitive checks where possible.
A combination appearing only in smoothies should not automatically establish suitability for roasted vegetables.
Novelty classes
    • common;
    • established;
    • uncommon;
    • rare;
    • not observed;
    • insufficient coverage.
Deliverables
    • Corpus ingestion or lookup process.
    • Reproducible novelty calculation.
    • Corpus metadata.
    • Tests for aliases, sparse data and misleading zero counts.
    • Scout UI showing evidence scope.
Exit condition
Scout can say exactly what “rare” means and where the claim came from.

Phase 8 — Add controlled tasting protocols
Goal
Turn hypotheses into kitchen experiments.
Work
Each Scout candidate should define:
    • transformed state;
    • candidate ingredient;
    • starting ratio;
    • smallest useful test;
    • success condition;
    • likely failure;
    • correction options;
    • safety note where relevant.
Example
Test one piece of roasted broccoli with three drops of blackcurrant vinegar and a small pinch of toasted sesame. Do not glaze the whole batch. Success means the acidity sharpens the browned broccoli without producing a jam-like fruit impression.
Deliverables
    • Tasting protocol schema.
    • Protocol renderer.
    • Result-entry form.
    • Verdict classes.
    • Notes for ratio and transformation dependence.
Verdict classes
    • works;
    • promising;
    • works only in this form;
    • needs adjustment;
    • dominated;
    • texturally wrong;
    • clashes;
    • inconclusive.
Exit condition
At least five Scout candidates have been physically tested and recorded.

