Phase 10 — Expand carefully
Goal
Increase coverage after the model has proved itself.
Ingredient expansion strategy
Prefer ingredients that stress different areas of the model:
    • mushroom — umami, moisture, meat analogies;
    • aubergine — bitterness, oil absorption, soft texture;
    • carrot — sweetness, raw versus roasted;
    • cucumber — fresh, salted, crushed and pickled states;
    • apple — sweet and savoury crossover;
    • beetroot — earthiness and sweetness;
    • beans — body, dryness and protein;
    • tofu — mild base and texture transformation.
Expansion rules
A new full ingredient should include:
    • several distinct transformed states;
    • sensory shifts;
    • at least two destinations;
    • at least two flavour routes;
    • risks;
    • transitions;
    • Cook evidence;
    • at least one potential Scout hypothesis.
Do not add an ingredient merely to increase a counter in the header.
Exit condition
New ingredients strengthen the reasoning model rather than only enlarging the catalogue.

Suggested first working sequence
When development resumes, the first practical sequence should be:
    1. Add the design and roadmap documents.
    2. Rename the current interface conceptually to Workshop.
    3. Choose broccoli or cabbage as the full pilot journey.
    4. Define preparation, state, flavour, texture, correction and destination vocabularies.
    5. Implement four complete journeys in text output.
    6. Replace universal plate completeness with destination-aware reasoning.
    7. Add six reusable flavour routes.
    8. Generate one Scout hypothesis from analogy rather than manual pairing.
    9. Check its occurrence in one recipe corpus.
    10. Cook and record the result.
That is the shortest credible path from the current repo to the intended project.

Things to postpone
Avoid spending the next rounds on:
    • dozens of new ingredients;
    • polished recipe generation;
    • user accounts;
    • shopping lists;
    • cloud infrastructure;
    • LLM chat;
    • embeddings;
    • flavour-compound scraping;
    • complex machine learning;
    • elaborate dashboards;
    • automatic weekly corpus refreshes.
Those may become useful later, but none answer the central question yet.

First milestone
Milestone name
One ingredient, two truths
Cook proof
Food-prep gives a useful, contextual path from a raw ingredient to a finished culinary destination.
Scout proof
Using the same structured knowledge, Food-prep identifies one uncommon combination, explains why it should work, confirms that it is rare in the searched evidence, and provides a controlled tasting method.
Completion criteria
    • One ingredient has four complete Cook journeys.
    • At least three destination types work.
    • At least three flavour routes work.
    • One hypothesis is generated through analogy.
    • Compatibility and novelty are shown separately.
    • The combination is physically tested.
    • The result is recorded honestly.
    • The README tells this story clearly.
Once that milestone exists, the project has a spine.
Everything after it is expansion rather than speculation.